---
import Next from '../components/Next.astro'
import Replay from '../components/Replay'
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro'
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import { capture } from '../lib/captures'
---

<Page
  page="test"
  title="Test"
  description="Publish your flows for QA to run with one click, then run the same checks in CI on every change."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">03 · Test</span>
      <h1 class="display">QA clicks it. CI runs it.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A request tests an API operation. A flow tests what users actually do, like booking an order.
      </p>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 12px">
        Publish your flows for QA to run with one click, then run the same checks in CI on every change.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" style="padding-top: 8px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="title">Assert what matters</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          By default, a request passes on any 2xx response. Add <code>assert</code> checks when you need more,
          and the response is judged entirely by them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use the familiar{' '}
          <a href="https://github.com/lukeed/uvu/blob/master/docs/api.assert.md">uvu/assert</a> helpers. Every
          check runs, and every result tells you what was expected and what actually came back.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/requests/petstore/find-pets.req.ts" region="request" />

      <h2 class="title">Let QA change the inputs</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A flow has <strong>inputs</strong>, like a GitHub workflow's inputs or Storybook's controls. QA changes
          them in a form before pressing Run, and CI takes the defaults or passes{' '}
          <code>--input name=value</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          An input can start from generated data. QA gets a realistic name without typing one, can press Generate
          for another, or press <strong>Run with defaults</strong> to skip every question.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="demo/sequences/new-pet.seq.ts" region="flow" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Inputs can repeat, too. Declare a traveler as a <code>c.object</code> and a booking's travelers as a{' '}
          <code>c.array</code> with a minimum and a maximum. QA sets how many there are and gets a row for each,
          and CI can pass the whole list with <code>--input travelers=[…]</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://demo.specbook.dev/#/seq/ui-demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Try it on the demo site ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="title">Give QA a portal</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>specbook build --minimal</code> publishes only the operations your requests and flows use. QA
          opens the portal, picks a request or a flow, chooses an environment and presses Run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><a class="btn primary" href="/qa/">Open the QA portal ↗</a></p>

      <h2 class="title">See exactly what failed</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When the API answers with something your checks did not expect, the run shows which check failed, what
          was expected, what came back, the line of your file and the request that led to it. Here an order has
          changed under a check that expected it to be placed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Replay client:visible capture={capture('test-fail')} />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A flow that fails once and passes on a retry is not hidden. With <code>--retries 1</code> it passes, is
          marked flaky in the summary and exits 0.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Replay client:visible capture={capture('test-flaky')} />

      <h2 class="title">Run it in CI too</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Add one workflow and CI runs your checks on every change.</p>
      </div>
      <Snippet file="snippets/test-workflow.yml" region="workflow" title="test.yml" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Secrets are environment variables named in the config, so your CI's secret store is all it needs. On
          GitHub Actions, failures are annotated on the change and summarised on the run. The exit code is 0 when
          everything passed, 1 when a flow failed or errored, 2 when the run could not start and 3 for a usage
          error.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap"><Next page="test" /></div>
</Page>
